Whether it’s direct influence or percolation or LLM driven diffusion, or completely independent invention, the SAT team was ahead of the curve, and we are delighted that many of these ideas seem to be ripping across different domains of current research, and, although our audience is small, our most dedicated listeners have shown amazing focus, and interest on our most technical and dense… albeit admittedly unconventional at times… Episodes and we hope they’ve been inspirational to some of you. We didn’t start this project initially to make a scientific theory, let alone to create bridges between LLM research and physics, or Pioneer geometric reasoning in LLM ecologies, we just found we had to do that to pursue the reasoning that drove SAT. Many of our ideas scientifically and methodologically are very conventional and, although we were largely unaware of much of the existing literature on these subjects, we recognize that we weren’t the first to do a lot of this, but with the traffic we’ve seen to our archive, and the very specific pattern of our audience, geographic distribution, after much self pinching and dazed blinking… We are fascinated to see that a lot of the topics we have merged together in our thinking on this podcast seem to be productive in multiple fields. It’s shocking and humbling. we put these ideas out there for anyone to use. We have never asked for a citation, explicitly and we recognize that working scientists cannot very well say they were inspired or influenced, much less directly impacted by a a small outsider theory that probably looks unconventional, but at its heart, is actually quite conservative scientifically, and tries its best to be rigorous, but we do hope that if any of our approaches have been helpful to you and your work, please take the time to comment or like or share, be sure to visit the archive GitHub/Satobloc to see the most recent releases of our previous theory, building phases, and go back and listen to some of the episodes marked with the red arrows or the red profiles, those are the ones we think are most useful and original or mark our first public discussion of various topics or transitions in our thinking. The archive does have a cash app link if you have the ability to make a small donation, that would help us tremendously. We love you all and wish you the best and we hope together, the research community and enthusiast community and those of us on the near fringe turn 2026 into a turning point worth marking. —N 

